    "atom::84a7316064bb585fd3c5eb48": {
      "atom_id": "atom::84a7316064bb585fd3c5eb48",
      "source_handle": "P0535",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "facet_contributions"
      ],
      "value": {
        "boundaries": "Focuses primarily on *intratumoral* bacteria, whereas the user's query specifies *gut* microbiome. However, the review notes connections between gut/oral commensals and intratumoral colonization (e.g., hematogenous spread of Fn), providing a bridge. Clinical evidence cited is largely from preclinical models or small human cohorts; large-scale prospective data specifically isolating gut vs. intra-tumor effects on ICI response is limited in this text.",
        "contribution": "Summarizes clinical associations between intratumoral microbial composition and ICI response in solid tumors (e.g., melanoma, NSCLC, CRC). Identifies specific taxa associated with better or worse outcomes (e.g., Bifidobacterium linked to improved anti-PD-L1 efficacy; Fn linked to resistance in MSS-CRC). Discusses the concept of 'microbiome-guided precision oncology' and potential for microbiome interventions to sensitize tumors to ICIs.",
        "facet_id": "F1"
      },
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::a808293b102fa5821c8a3af3eecbf34c4f6d97706d9adcf2e6fde0fc4f12038b",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "facet_contributions",
            "0"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::5639ec1abbb649da12f1466205d901fc5385c532b0d7fedd36387dbd191f0de1",
      "multiple_values_in_field": true
    },
    "atom::d0abf1e82b9862bbc30ded36": {
      "atom_id": "atom::d0abf1e82b9862bbc30ded36",
      "source_handle": "P0535",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "facet_contributions",
        "possible_uses"
      ],
      "value": "Can be used to contextualize the broader impact of microbiota on ICI efficacy, highlighting that local tumor ecology plays a critical role alongside systemic gut flora. Provides examples of successful microbiome-ICI combinations in preclinical settings.",
      "role": "planning",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::a808293b102fa5821c8a3af3eecbf34c4f6d97706d9adcf2e6fde0fc4f12038b",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "facet_contributions",
            "0",
            "possible_uses"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::7bc6899dc75b629eebda875662d86788f9851cdca9ac68a03c210454442681eb",
      "multiple_values_in_field": true
    },
    "atom::d36f9ec6bddaf6a75ddd347b": {
      "atom_id": "atom::d36f9ec6bddaf6a75ddd347b",
      "source_handle": "P0535",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "facet_contributions"
      ],
      "value": {
        "boundaries": "Mechanisms described are often derived from animal models or in vitro studies. The review acknowledges that many associations are correlative and causal validation in humans is ongoing. The distinction between direct bacterial effects and indirect effects mediated by other gut-derived factors is sometimes blurred in the synthesis.",
        "contribution": "Details specific molecular mechanisms by which intratumoral bacteria modulate ICI efficacy. Mechanisms include: 1) Direct immune checkpoint regulation (Fn upregulating PD-L1 via Dps protein); 2) Metabolic modulation (Fn-derived succinate suppressing cGAS-IFN-β pathway; L. reuteri-derived I3A activating AhR to boost CD8+ T cell IFN-γ production); 3) Immune cell recruitment/polarization (recruiting MDSCs or Tregs via chemokines like CXCL1/CXCL8); 4) STING pathway activation (Bifidobacterium activating STING to convert non-responders to responders). Also describes how engineered bacteria or probiotics can reverse resistance.",
        "facet_id": "F2"
      },
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::a808293b102fa5821c8a3af3eecbf34c4f6d97706d9adcf2e6fde0fc4f12038b",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "facet_contributions",
            "1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::5639ec1abbb649da12f1466205d901fc5385c532b0d7fedd36387dbd191f0de1",
      "multiple_values_in_field": true
    },
    "atom::ce6a6d25bf32c77948c0d650": {
      "atom_id": "atom::ce6a6d25bf32c77948c0d650",
      "source_handle": "P0535",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "facet_contributions",
        "possible_uses"
      ],
      "value": "Provides detailed mechanistic pathways (metabolic, signaling, cellular) that can be compared with gut microbiome-mediated mechanisms. Helps identify common themes (e.g., metabolic cross-talk, STING activation) across different microbial compartments.",
      "role": "planning",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::a808293b102fa5821c8a3af3eecbf34c4f6d97706d9adcf2e6fde0fc4f12038b",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "facet_contributions",
            "1",
            "possible_uses"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::7bc6899dc75b629eebda875662d86788f9851cdca9ac68a03c210454442681eb",
      "multiple_values_in_field": true
    },
    "atom::f6d3453cbc1d3516978ae25d": {
      "atom_id": "atom::f6d3453cbc1d3516978ae25d",
      "source_handle": "P0535",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "planning_summary"
      ],
      "value": "This review provides extensive material on how intratumoral bacteria (distinct from but related to gut microbiome) modulate the efficacy of immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) in solid tumors. It details specific mechanisms of immune suppression (e.g., Fn-induced PD-L1, MDSC recruitment) and immune enhancement (e.g., Bifidobacterium-STING axis), offering mechanistic insights and clinical correlations relevant to Facet F2. It also discusses therapeutic strategies targeting these microbes to overcome ICI resistance, providing context for Facet F1.",
      "role": "planner_interpretation",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::a808293b102fa5821c8a3af3eecbf34c4f6d97706d9adcf2e6fde0fc4f12038b",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "planning_summary"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::9f551625eaeb26ce413544e3b764d133b6a0cff60afe33037a597849bc277a83",
      "multiple_values_in_field": false
    },
    "atom::44a2fa1a46e0fdd814aa8881": {
      "atom_id": "atom::44a2fa1a46e0fdd814aa8881",
      "source_handle": "P0535",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "scope_interpretation_cautions"
      ],
      "value": "The review focuses on *intratumoral* bacteria, which may differ from the *gut* microbiome specified in the user's query. While there are links (e.g., translocation), they are distinct ecological niches. Use with caution when generalizing gut-specific mechanisms to intra-tumor effects.",
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::a808293b102fa5821c8a3af3eecbf34c4f6d97706d9adcf2e6fde0fc4f12038b",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "scope_interpretation_cautions",
            "0"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::25284f4a980d127083de564424afdb5ea4ccd51716b7d6d60ab627dffed7afc7",
      "multiple_values_in_field": true
    },
    "atom::1d685375e8722d8afbd25341": {
      "atom_id": "atom::1d685375e8722d8afbd25341",
      "source_handle": "P0535",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "scope_interpretation_cautions"
      ],
      "value": "Many mechanistic claims are based on preclinical models (mice/cell lines). Human clinical evidence is often correlative or from small cohorts. Do not overstate causal certainty for human outcomes.",
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::a808293b102fa5821c8a3af3eecbf34c4f6d97706d9adcf2e6fde0fc4f12038b",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "scope_interpretation_cautions",
            "1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::25284f4a980d127083de564424afdb5ea4ccd51716b7d6d60ab627dffed7afc7",
      "multiple_values_in_field": true
    },
    "atom::07d1a824dccc99281c19dc7d": {
      "atom_id": "atom::07d1a824dccc99281c19dc7d",
      "source_handle": "P0535",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "scope_interpretation_cautions"
      ],
      "value": "The review emphasizes context-dependency; the same bacterium can have opposite effects in different cancer types or immune contexts. Avoid universal statements about specific taxa without specifying the tumor type and immune status.",
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::a808293b102fa5821c8a3af3eecbf34c4f6d97706d9adcf2e6fde0fc4f12038b",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "scope_interpretation_cautions",
            "2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::25284f4a980d127083de564424afdb5ea4ccd51716b7d6d60ab627dffed7afc7",
